WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.825
At our sites, the supervisors carry and operate

00:07.825 --> 00:12.050
the ALTAIR four-X gas monitor. It reads four things:

00:12.050 --> 00:15.287
oxygen, flammable gas, carbon monoxide,

00:15.287 --> 00:18.138
and hydrogen sulfide. It's a warning tool and a

00:18.138 --> 00:20.875
measuring tool. It tells a trained person what's

00:20.875 --> 00:23.462
in the air at the spot where the monitor is.

00:24.588 --> 00:27.426
You won't be asked to run one as a new miner.

00:27.426 --> 00:29.501
What you do need to know is what it looks and sounds

00:29.501 --> 00:32.201
like when it alarms, and what you do next.

00:33.318 --> 00:35.781
When the monitor alarms, it does three things at

00:35.781 --> 00:38.793
once. It sounds a loud audible alarm,

00:38.793 --> 00:41.968
it flashes its alarm lights, and it vibrates.

00:41.968 --> 00:44.980
If you hear one, or see one go off on a coworker,

00:44.980 --> 00:47.918
a vehicle, or a supervisor's belt, treat it as

00:47.918 --> 00:51.506
real. Your site decides the exact alarm levels,

00:51.506 --> 00:53.843
and your site-specific training covers them.

00:54.973 --> 00:58.035
Two limits matter. Only people your company designates

00:58.035 --> 01:01.285
operate the monitor, test it, and calibrate it.

01:01.285 --> 01:03.760
If you see one that looks damaged, wet,

01:03.760 --> 01:06.285
or isn't working, tell your supervisor.

01:06.285 --> 01:09.510
And the four-gas monitor doesn't read every gas.

01:09.510 --> 01:12.322
It doesn't read nitrogen dioxide from blasting,

01:12.322 --> 01:15.335
for example. A quiet monitor is good news,

01:15.335 --> 01:18.398
but it isn't proof that the air is safe from everything,

01:18.398 --> 01:20.735
so keep paying attention to how you feel and to

01:20.735 --> 01:22.160
what's going on around you.

01:25.474 --> 01:28.724
Your response is four words. Stop. Warn.

01:28.724 --> 01:30.374
Withdraw. Report.

01:31.505 --> 01:33.655
Stop what you're doing. Warn the people around

01:33.655 --> 01:37.142
you, loudly, so everyone hears. Withdraw from the

01:37.142 --> 01:39.667
area, by the way your site has told you,

01:39.667 --> 01:42.717
toward fresh air. And report to your supervisor,

01:42.717 --> 01:45.330
or on the radio once you're clear, what you heard

01:45.330 --> 01:46.430
and where you were.

01:47.555 --> 01:49.392
There are four things you never do.

01:49.392 --> 01:51.830
Don't silence the alarm and carry on.

01:51.830 --> 01:54.055
Don't go closer to see what set it off.

01:54.055 --> 01:56.555
Don't try to smell the air; some of these gases

01:56.555 --> 01:59.030
can't be smelled, and some can't be trusted to

01:59.030 --> 02:01.817
warn you. And don't make anything that could ignite

02:01.817 --> 02:05.817
gas, like smoking, welding, or striking a spark,

02:05.817 --> 02:07.405
in an area that's alarming.

02:08.549 --> 02:10.849
Sometimes there's no alarm, and you still feel

02:10.849 --> 02:13.911
wrong. Dizziness, a headache, nausea,

02:13.911 --> 02:16.474
or trouble catching your breath can be early signs

02:16.474 --> 02:19.036
of bad air. Don't wait for a monitor to confirm

02:19.036 --> 02:22.299
it. Withdraw to fresh air and tell your supervisor.

02:22.299 --> 02:24.948
Your body can warn you before the monitor does,

02:24.948 --> 02:27.624
particularly if the monitor isn't near you.

02:28.751 --> 02:30.863
It helps to know where trouble comes from.

02:30.863 --> 02:33.888
A stopped fan, a damaged stopping or door,

02:33.888 --> 02:36.888
or any change in airflow can alter the air quickly.

02:36.888 --> 02:40.476
Fires, smoldering belts or tires, and engine exhaust

02:40.476 --> 02:44.163
make carbon monoxide. Low or enclosed spaces,

02:44.163 --> 02:48.001
like sumps, pits, and tanks, can hold gases or

02:48.001 --> 02:51.113
lack oxygen. And a shot, as you saw in the last

02:51.113 --> 02:54.726
lesson, leaves fumes behind. If any of those happen,

02:54.726 --> 02:57.701
report it, even if no alarm has gone off.

02:58.865 --> 03:01.203
Here's how it might go. You're working a heading,

03:01.203 --> 03:03.865
and the supervisor's monitor starts to alarm.

03:03.865 --> 03:06.503
You stop what you're doing. You say loudly,

03:06.503 --> 03:09.328
"Gas alarm, we're withdrawing." You make sure your

03:09.328 --> 03:11.541
partner hears you. You go together,

03:11.541 --> 03:14.378
by the route you were trained on, to fresh air.

03:14.378 --> 03:16.691
You check in with your supervisor on the radio,

03:16.691 --> 03:19.166
and you stay out until you're told otherwise.

03:19.166 --> 03:21.266
Nobody goes back to see what it was.

03:24.590 --> 03:27.653
If the alarm comes with smoke, signs of a fire,

03:27.653 --> 03:30.265
a carbon monoxide alarm in an emergency,

03:30.265 --> 03:33.153
or stench gas, that's an escape situation.

03:33.153 --> 03:36.353
At the first sign of smoke, a carbon monoxide alarm,

03:36.353 --> 03:40.315
or stench gas, put on your W65 right away and escape

03:40.315 --> 03:43.140
through your escapeway, the way you were trained.

03:43.140 --> 03:46.915
The W65 protects against carbon monoxide only.

03:46.915 --> 03:49.853
It doesn't make oxygen. We cover it fully in Module

03:49.853 --> 03:50.640
3.

03:51.752 --> 03:54.552
If a coworker is down in an area that's alarming,

03:54.552 --> 03:57.740
don't rush in. A rescuer who collapses is a second

03:57.740 --> 04:00.715
victim. Call for help right away, say where you

04:00.715 --> 04:03.377
are and what you see, and let the trained people

04:03.377 --> 04:05.127
go in with the right equipment.

04:08.455 --> 04:11.342
To sum up. A supervisor carries the monitor,

04:11.342 --> 04:14.505
but anyone can hear it. Treat an alarm as real.

04:14.505 --> 04:18.505
Stop, warn, withdraw, report. Don't silence it,

04:18.505 --> 04:21.280
don't investigate, and don't trust your nose.

04:21.280 --> 04:24.405
If it comes with smoke or a carbon monoxide alarm,

04:24.405 --> 04:27.992
put on your W65 and escape. And never walk into

04:27.992 --> 04:30.555
bad air after someone. Call for help.

04:31.668 --> 04:34.643
Your site's plans always override this video.
